NARR = [
"Every sample the ADS1299 takes is a comparison. The converter measures your signal against a reference voltage, like measuring something with a ruler. If the ruler stretches and shrinks, even a perfectly steady signal gives wobbling readings. Your brain signal is only microvolts, so this ruler has to be extremely steady. That's what this section of the datasheet is about: how the chip makes its ruler, and how you keep it steady.",
"The reference also sets how big a signal the chip can measure. The full-scale range is plus or minus the reference divided by the gain. With a 4.5 volt reference and a gain of 24, that's plus or minus 187.5 millivolts. The converter splits that range into about 16.8 million steps, so each step is just 0.022 microvolts.",
"Inside the chip, a tiny, very stable voltage called a bandgap is amplified by a buffer up to exactly 4.5 volts. Three internal resistors, 9.8, 13.4 and 36.85 kilo-ohms, are trimmed at the factory to make it exact. The 4.5 volts is made relative to AVSS, so when you use the internal reference, you connect VREFN to AVSS. The two big capacitors in the picture are yours to add: 100 microfarads on VCAP1, and 10 microfarads between VREFP and VREFN. There's also an external reference option, but you don't need it; the Cyton uses the internal one.",
"On your board, AVSS is minus 2.5 volts. The reference sits 4.5 volts above it, so VREFN is minus 2.5 volts, and VREFP is plus 2.0 volts. VREFP stays below AVDD, which is plus 2.5 volts. In a single-supply design, with AVSS at zero, VREFP would simply be 4.5 volts.",
"The capacitors do a second job. Together with the chip's internal resistors, they form a slow low-pass filter on the reference, which cleans the ruler. The datasheet says that for high-end EEG, the values should limit the reference bandwidth to under 10 hertz, so the reference noise doesn't dominate your system noise. That's why these capacitors are so big.",
"One more detail about VCAP1, from page 70. Ceramic capacitors of the X7R or X5R kind are piezoelectric: tap them or shake them, and they produce a small voltage. On VCAP1, that noise goes straight into your reference. So in a system that vibrates, the datasheet suggests a tantalum or C0G capacitor there. The Cyton uses an X5R ceramic, so this is a choice you'll need to make for your board.",
"Here's a trap for your firmware. When the chip powers up, it expects an external reference. In the CONFIG3 register, bit 7, called PD_REFBUF, starts at zero, which leaves the internal reference off. Your firmware must set that bit to one. The Cyton does it at start-up by writing hex E C to CONFIG3, on both chips when a Daisy is attached. That same write also turns on the bias buffer, and selects the internal bias reference, halfway between AVDD and AVSS.",
"And one more thing about timing. The big capacitor on VCAP1 charges slowly. The datasheet says to send the reset pulse only after the power-up delay, or after VCAP1 has reached 1.1 volts, whichever takes longer. Only then do you program the registers, and CONFIG3 is the one that turns on the reference.",
"To sum up. The reference is the ruler for every sample, 4.5 volts above AVSS. Connect VREFN to AVSS, add at least 10 microfarads between VREFP and VREFN, and 100 microfarads on VCAP1. Choose that VCAP1 capacitor with vibration in mind. And in firmware, wait for VCAP1, reset, then set the reference buffer bit in CONFIG3 before you start recording.",
]
TITLES = ["The reference is a ruler", "The ruler sets the range", "Making 4.5 V inside the chip", "On ±2.5 V supplies", "Keeping the ruler quiet", "The VCAP1 vibration warning", "Turning it on in firmware", "Start-up timing", "Summary"]
